<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mithridates I of Parthia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 171–132 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Mithridates I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mithridates I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> from about 171 to 132 BCE and is regarded as the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> as a major power. He conquered Media and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. His victories reduced the Seleucids to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and established Parthia as the dominant Iranian state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia.jpg" alt="Mithridates I of Parthia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Mithradates_I_of_Parthia,_Seleucia_mint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mithridates was the son of Priapatius and the great-nephew of the first Arsacid king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsaces-i">Arsaces I</a>. His name is the Greek form of the Iranian Mihrdāt, meaning given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a>. Parthian custom required a ruler to be succeeded by his own son, but his elder brother Phraates I broke the custom and named Mithridates as successor. The Roman historian Justin says Phraates had noticed his competence.</p><p>The kingdom he inherited was one of several medium-sized powers that had arisen as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> declined. It covered present-day Khorasan Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcania">Hyrcania</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the south of present-day Turkmenistan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Mithridates first attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a>, weakened by wars with its neighbours. Between 163 and 155 BCE he took Aria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i">Eucratides I</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> became a Parthian stronghold. Turning west, he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a> in 148 or 147 BCE and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/media-atropatene">Media Atropatene</a>. In 141 BCE he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, minted coins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> and held an investiture ceremony there. Elymais and Characene became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and Parthian authority reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>.</p><p>In about 140 BCE, while he fought the Saka in the east, the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-nicator">Demetrius II Nicator</a> tried to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. He was captured in 138 BCE and sent to a palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcania">Hyrcania</a>, where Mithridates treated him hospitably and married him to his daughter Rhodogune. Mithridates then punished Elymais for helping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><p>He was the first Parthian king to take the Achaemenid title King of Kings. His later coins show a Hellenistic diadem and the title Philhellene, friend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, aimed at his new Greek subjects. He renamed Nisa as Mithradatkert, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> was founded in his time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mithridates died in about 132 BCE and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phraates-ii">Phraates II</a>. Historians such as Klaus Schippmann argue that his campaigns were more than raids for plunder and aimed at reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Homa Katouzian has compared him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mithridates I of Parthia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Eucratides is said to have broken a siege by Demetrius, king of the Indians, who came with 60,000 men, but he was later repulsed by Menander I and defeated by the Parthian king <b>Mithridates I</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The small regional kingdom became a major imperial power under <b>Mithridates I</b> (r. c. 171–132 BCE), who conquered Media and, around 141 BCE, the Seleucid heartland of Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mithridates I of Parthia</b> exploited the succession struggles that followed Antiochus IV's death to conquer Media (c. 148–147 BCE) and Mesopotamia (141 BCE), capturing Seleucia-on-Tigris, the empire's eastern capital, and in 138 BCE taking the Seleucid king Demetrius II prisoner.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
